pat (devex): Heads-up: hit a weird duplicate webhook / order in the onboarding sampleapp when a 429 + auth expiry happened together. Sharing a short repro and asking for a practical SDK pattern to avoid this.

indra (sdk): short Q: are we talking server-to-server webhook or client-side replay?

pat (devex): client-side. Flow: user submits -> frontend POST /create -> network returns 429 then token refresh kicks in -> frontend schedules retry with exponential backoff + small jitter -> meanwhile server eventually processes original and emits a webhook to our backend -> our backend sees webhook and creates local record, then frontend retry also POSTs and backend creates duplicate because idempotency keys didn't line up.

frank (platform): interesting mix. Two separate systems involved: (A) client retries after 429 and (B) async webhook processing creating resource. If webhook processing and client replay both create the canonical resource, we end up with duplicates unless we have server-wide idempotency coordination.

pat (devex): timeline (simplified): t0 client POST /orders (with idempotency key K) -> t1 429 returned -> client schedules retry at t2 -> t1.5 auth refresh executes -> t1.6 server eventually accepts original (late) and emits webhook to backend -> backend creates order A linked to K-old? -> t2 client retry POST uses either same K or new K depending on client logic -> backend sees new attempt and creates order B -> duplicate.

indra (sdk): does the client reuse the same idempotency key after 429?

pat (devex): sometimes yes, sometimes we generate new key on retry (we had inconsistent behavior across sampleapp modules).

frank (platform): several mitigation layers: 1) client: prefer reusing same idempotency key for retries until you decisively decide to create a fresh attempt; 2) server: provide an idempotency lookup API and webhook payload should include original idempotency key so downstream systems can correlate; 3) backend: adopt a unified dedupe store for operations triggered by direct API and webhook.

indra (sdk): SDK-level practicality: add a small helper that implements a safe-replay strategy for 429s when webhooks/backends are in play. Proposal: `sdk.replayWithWebhookAwareness(pending, opts)` which does: - reuse original key for the first N retries - before sending a retry check `HEAD /orders/idemp/{key}` to detect if server already created the order via webhook - if HEAD returns 200 (complete) then short-circuit and fetch resource instead of re-POST - if HEAD 202 (in-flight) poll status - if HEAD 404 then proceed with retry. Also expose config: reuseKeyAttempts, graceWindowMs.

pat (devex): can you sketch pseudocode? will add to sampleapp.

indra (sdk): sure, pseudocode we can include in docs: ```
// persisted pending: {idempotencyKey, endpoint, retries}
async function replayWithWebhookAwareness(pending) {
  for (let i=0; i < opts.reuseKeyAttempts; i++) {
    const status = await sdk.idempotency.check(pending.idempotencyKey)
    if (status === 'complete') return sdk.fetchByIdempotency(pending.idempotencyKey)
    if (status === 'in_progress') await pollStatus(pending.idempotencyKey)
    // small jitter + backoff before retry
    await sleep(rand(0, base * Math.pow(2, i)))
    // reuse the same idempotency key for the attempt
    const resp = await sdk.post(pending.endpoint, pending.body, {idempotencyKey: pending.idempotencyKey})
    if (resp.ok) return resp.payload
  }
  // fallback: create fresh key and include header pointing to original
  const newKey = uuid()
  const resp = await sdk.post(pending.endpoint, pending.body, {idempotencyKey: newKey, headers: {'X-Original-Idempotency': pending.idempotencyKey}})
  return resp.payload
}
```

frank (platform): also server-side: ensure webhook payload includes the original client-provided idempotency key and the canonical resource id. That lets backend correlate incoming webhooks with pending client attempts. If your architecture can't change the webhook, consider adding a reconciliation API endpoint `POST /reconcile` that accepts `originalIdempotency` and returns canonical resource if present.

pat (devex): what about jitter type? we used a small decorrelated jitter and still saw collisions when many clients retried after tokens rotated.

indra (sdk): for this scenario I'd recommend full jitter for 429 retries (sleep = rand(0, base * 2^attempt)). That reduces synchronized bursts. Also make reuseKeyAttempts small (1-2) and rely on idempotency-check before retry to avoid extra hits.

frank (platform): one more edge: if your clients reuse key and the server's idempotency retention window is short, a delayed original may be outside dedupe window and you'll still create a duplicate. So document server retention guarantees and choose grace windows accordingly.

pat (devex): good point. For the sampleapp we'll: 1) persist minimal pending metadata (no raw body if possible), 2) default to reuse key for the first retry, 3) call idempotency HEAD before retrying, 4) use full jitter backoff, 5) include X-Original-Idempotency header when escalating to a new key, and 6) require webhook to include original key in payload.

indra (sdk): I'll draft a tiny JS helper and a README snippet. I also propose adding a lint rule in the sampleapp to prevent persisting bodies and to enforce consistent idempotency reuse behavior.

deploy-bot: sampleapps/onboarding #631 pipeline succeeded :white_check_mark:

pat (devex): thanks both. I'll open a PR with the helper and an e2e that simulates 429 + auth expiry + delayed webhook so we catch regressions. Tagging PR with "sdk-webhook-idempotency".

indra (sdk): awesome. When PR lands I'll add the helper to SDK docs and a short entry in the quickstart about jitter/backoff choices for 429s when webhooks are part of the flow.

frank (platform): and I'll draft a small server-side compatibility note for webhook payloads including idempotency keys and dedupe window expectations.

pat (devex): perfect, thanks :thumbsup:
